Ten years between life and death—both lost in haze;
I try not to think of you, yet I cannot forget.
Your lonely grave lies a thousand miles away;
there is nowhere to speak of this desolation.

A new song, a cup of wine;
Last year’s weather, the old pavilion.
The sun sinks in the west—when will it return?
Helplessly, the blossoms fall away;
As if we’ve met before, the swallows return.
Alone, I pace the fragrant path in the little garden.

A new song, a cup of wine;
Last year’s weather, the old pavilion.
How soon will the setting sun return?
Helpless, the flowers fall away;
Familiar, it seems, the swallows come again.
Alone, I wander the fragrant path of the little garden.

A song of new-made words, a cup of wine;
Last year’s weather, the same old pavilions.
The sun sinks west—when will it return?
Nothing can stop the flowers falling away;
As if known before, the swallows return.
Alone I linger on the small garden’s fragrant path.

A song of new words, a cup of wine;
Last year’s weather, the same old pavilion.
The sun sinks west—when will it return?
Nothing can stop the flowers falling;
The swallows return, as though seen before.
Alone I linger on the small garden’s fragrant path.

A song with new words, a cup of wine;
Last year's weather, the same old terraces.
The sun sinks west—when will it return?
Nothing can stop the flowers from falling;
The swallows return, as though known long before.
Alone, I wander the small garden's fragrant path.

A new song, a cup of wine;
Last year's weather, the same old pavilion.
The sun sinks west—when will it return?
Helpless, I watch the blossoms fall;
The swallows return, as though known before.
Alone I wander the little garden's fragrant path.

A song with newly written words, a cup of wine;
The weather as it was last year, the same old terraces and pavilions.
The sun sinks in the west—when will it return?
Nothing can be done: the blossoms fall away;
As if known before, the swallows return.
Alone I linger on the fragrant path in the little garden.

A new song’s words, a cup of wine;
Last year’s weather, the same old pavilion.
The sun sinks west—when will it return?
Helpless, I watch the blossoms fall;
As if once known, the swallows come again.
Alone I wander the small garden’s fragrant path.
